{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the distance XY where X and Y are the second intersections of two circumcircles with the altitude line from A.\n\n## Setup\n\nPlace $B=(0,0)$, $C=(14,0)$. From $AB=15$, $AC=13$:\n- $x^2+y^2=225$ and $(x-14)^2+y^2=169$ gives $x=9$, $y=12$.\n- So $A=(9,12)$.\n\nLine $l$ (perpendicular to $BC$ through $A$) is $x=9$.\n\n**Circumcenter:** Perpendicular bisector of $BC$ is $x=7$. The perpendicular bisector of $AB$ (midpoint $(4.5,6)$, slope $-3/4$) at $x=7$ gives $y=33/8$. So $O=(7,33/8)$, and $R=65/8$ (verified via $R=\\frac{abc}{4\\Delta}=\\frac{13\\cdot14\\cdot15}{4\\cdot84}=\\frac{65}{8}$).\n\n## Circle through $A$, $O$, $B$\n\nCircle $x^2+y^2+Dx+Ey+F=0$ with $F=0$ (through origin). Solving with $A$ and $O$:\n$$D=\\tfrac{29}{10},\\quad E=-\\tfrac{837}{40}$$\n\nIntersect with $x=9$: $y^2-\\tfrac{837}{40}y+\\tfrac{1071}{10}=0$. One root is $y=12$ (point $A$); product of roots $=\\tfrac{1071}{10}$, so the other root is:\n$$y_X = \\frac{1071/10}{12}=\\frac{357}{40}$$\n\nThus $X=\\left(9,\\tfrac{357}{40}\\right)$.\n\n## Circle through $A$, $O$, $C$\n\nSolving with $A$, $O$, $C=(14,0)$:\n$$D=-\\tfrac{53}{2},\\quad E=-\\tfrac{323}{24},\\quad F=175$$\n\nIntersect with $x=9$: $y^2-\\tfrac{323}{24}y+\\tfrac{35}{2}=0$. One root is $y=12$; product $=\\tfrac{35}{2}$, so:\n$$y_Y = \\frac{35/2}{12}=\\frac{35}{24}$$\n\nThus $Y=\\left(9,\\tfrac{35}{24}\\right)$.\n\n## Computing $XY$\n\nBoth points lie on $x=9$, so:\n$$XY=\\frac{357}{40}-\\frac{35}{24}=\\frac{357\\cdot 24-35\\cdot 40}{960}=\\frac{8568-1400}{960}=\\frac{7168}{960}=\\frac{112}{15}$$\n\nThis is irreducible ($\\gcd(112,15)=1$), so $a=112$, $b=15$.\n\n$$a+b = 112+15 = \\boxed{127}$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this geometry problem. Triangle ABC with AB=15, AC=13, BC=14, circumcenter O. Line l through A perpendicular to BC. Circumcircle of AOB meets l at X (other than A), circumcircle of AOC meets l at Y (other than A). Find XY.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Place BC on x-axis. Let B=(0,0), C=(14,0). A is such that AB=15, AC=13.\n\nA=(x,y): x²+y²=225, (x-14)²+y²=169. Subtract: x²-(x-14)²=56 → x²-x²+28x-196=56 → 28x=252 → x=9. y²=225-81=144 → y=12. So A=(9,12).\n\nLine l through A perpendicular to BC (x-axis) is the vertical line x=9.\n\nCircumcenter O: perpendicular bisector of BC is x=7. Perpendicular bisector of AB: midpoint (4.5,6), slope of AB is 12/9=4/3, perp slope -3/4. Line: y-6 = -3/4(x-4.5). At x=7: y-6=-3/4(2.5)=-7.5/4=-1.875. y=4.125=33/8. So O=(7,33/8).\n\nCircumradius R = distance from O to B = √(49+(33/8)²)=√(49+1089/64)=√((3136+1089)/64)=√(4225/64)=65/8.\n\nCheck: area = (1/2)(14)(12)=84. R = abc/(4·area) = 13·14·15/(4·84)=2730/336=65/8. ✓\n\nNow circumcircle of AOB. Points A=(9,12), O=(7,33/8), B=(0,0).\n\nGeneral circle: x²+y²+Dx+Ey+F=0. Through B=(0,0): F=0. Through A=(9,12): 81+144+9D+12E=0 → 9D+12E=-225. Through O=(7,33/8): 49+1089/64+7D+(33/8)E=0 → (3136+1089)/64+7D+(33/8)E=0 → 4225/64+7D+(33/8)E=0 → 7D+(33/8)E=-4225/64.\n\nFrom first: 9D+12E=-225 → D=(-225-12E)/9=-25-(4/3)E.\n\nSub: 7(-25-(4/3)E)+(33/8)E=-4225/64 → -175-(28/3)E+(33/8)E=-4225/64 → -175+E(-28/3+33/8)=-4225/64.\n\n-28/3+33/8 = (-224+99)/24 = -125/24.\n\nSo -175-(125/24)E=-4225/64 → -(125/24)E=-4225/64+175=-4225/64+11200/64=6975/64.\n\nE = -(6975/64)(24/125) = -(6975·24)/(64·125) = -(6975·24)/8000.\n\n6975/125=55.8, let me compute: 6975·24=167400. 167400/8000=20.925. So E=-20.925=-837/40? Let me check: 20.925=837/40? 837/40=20.925. Yes. Hmm let me recompute. 167400/8000 = 1674/80 = 837/40. So E=-837/40.\n\nD=-25-(4/3)(-837/40)=-25+(4·837)/(120)=-25+3348/120=-25+27.9=2.9=29/10.\n\nSo circle AOB: x²+y²+(29/10)x-(837/40)y=0.\n\nIntersect with l: x=9. 81+y²+(29/10)(9)-(837/40)y=0 → y²-(837/40)y+81+261/10=0 → y²-(837/40)y+(81+26.1)=0 → y²-(837/40)y+1071/10=0.\n\n81+261/10 = 810/10+261/10=1071/10.\n\nOne root is y=12 (point A). Check: 144-(837/40)(12)+1071/10